#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Oracle-against-Blaze matrix for query, cquery and aquery output formats and flags.

usage: scripts/query/compare --binary PATH --label NAME [--project P[,P...]]
                             [--command query,cquery,aquery] [--cells REGEX] [--no-run]
                             [--details N]
       scripts/query/compare cells [--project P] [--command C]   list the matrix, run nothing
       scripts/query/compare selftest
       scripts/query/compare help

Projects: brotli, re2 (copies of the read-only checkouts in ~/.cache/blaze/corpus), probe (a
small generated workspace with select()s, package groups and odd characters), workerd (a sample
of cells through scripts/workerd/run, read-only use). Default: brotli,re2,probe.

Each cell is one command line (expression x --output x flags). For every cell:
  oracle A   the oracle (~/.cache/blaze/oracle/bazel-9.3.2-linux-x86_64) in one server
  oracle B   the oracle again after `shutdown` (a new JVM): Bazel's own run-to-run variation
             (identity hash order in Digraph, HashSet<Target>) shows up as A != B
  blaze      the binary under test
Workspaces: ~/.cache/blaze/work/query-cmp/{o,b}/<project> (rsync of the checkout minus bazel-*
and .git, refreshed at the start of a run); output user roots ~/.cache/blaze/oracle-out/qc-{o,b}.
The two sides' paths have equal lengths, and Blaze's workspace, output base and install base are
replaced by the oracle's in Blaze's stdout (byte for byte, so binary protos stay comparable).
workerd uses the copies of scripts/workerd/run (~/.cache/blaze/work/workerd and workerd-blaze,
output user roots ur-workerd and ur-workerd-blaze, MemoryMax 20G), run here directly through
compare_common's launcher with the arguments scripts/workerd/run would pass; its paths are
replaced as text only.

Verdict per cell:
  identical            same exit code and same stdout bytes as oracle A or B
  identical-unordered  the oracle itself varies (A != B), or the cell's order comes from Java
                       identity hashes (see order_is_identity_hashed); Blaze's lines (and the
                       items of a factored graph node) equal A's as a multiset
  oracle-varies        the oracle's two runs differ beyond line order (e.g. aquery's streamed
                       formats number ids in parallel), Blaze has the same exit code and
                       matches neither: no byte comparison is defined
  loud                 Blaze stopped at an `unported!` ("not ported yet") or panicked
  different            anything else (stdout or exit code differ)
  oracle-varies        the oracle varies and Blaze matches neither of its runs
Results: ~/.cache/blaze/query-compare/<label>/{results.json,matrix.md,cells/<id>/...}.
--no-run re-classifies the saved outputs. Run it under systemd-run (AGENTS.md: heavy jobs).

Every verdict also requires the whole stderr to equal oracle A's or B's after the exclusion
rules of scripts/lib/compare_common.py (VOLATILE_RULES; `paths`: each side's workspace, output
base and install base; B1_GAP_RULES: lines that depend on the oracle's warm server, which runs
all cells of a project), listed in the report with the lines each changed or removed.

Guards (scripts/lib/compare_common.py). Every command, workerd's included, runs in a --scope unit
(clean environment HOME and PATH=/usr/bin:/bin) under a supervisor that records how it really
ended (`exit N`, `signal NAME`, `oom-kill`, `timeout`), saved as <side>.term and compared
instead of the exit code (a cell saved without .term is SKIPPED); the terminations of `info` and
`shutdown` are listed (an abnormal one fails the run). The oracle must be the pinned one
(UPSTREAM.json). Each oracle output has oracleA.key/oracleB.key, written after the output files
and holding their digests with the key (oracle sha256, the argv that ran, the environment, the
digest of the project's input: the corpus checkout, the probe files, or workerd's git HEAD and
status). Each Blaze output has blaze.capture.json (the binary's sha256 and the files' digests).
--no-run re-classifies stored outputs: an oracle output whose key or files differ, and a Blaze
output not made by the binary named with --binary, are SKIPPED, and a --no-run run never exits 0
(it judges nothing new). The stored outputs are raw bytes, so this tool's own sha256 is not part
of the key.
Each cell gets one compare_common class: IDENTICAL (identical, the oracle exited 0 or
DECLARED_EXIT declares its code, stderr equal), IDENTICAL-REDUCED (identical-unordered: stdout
compared as a multiset of lines), KNOWN-GAP (stderr equal except in B1 lines), BOTH-FAILED
(identical but both failed, not declared), NOT-PORTED (unported!), DIFFERENT (different,
oracle-varies, a panic, stderr), SKIPPED (no saved result, no termination, stale oracle output,
Blaze output of another binary). matrix.md shows these classes, skipped cells included. The
output and results.json carry the repository HEAD and dirty flag, both binaries' paths and
sha256 and the denominator. Exit status: 0 only when at least one cell was compared and every
selected cell is IDENTICAL; 1 otherwise (an empty selection or --no-run included); 2 on a usage
error or a refusal.
selftest drives this command line with fake binaries (compare_common.selftest_tool).
"""
